## Describing a number

### Two questions, always

For any numeric variable, ask:

1.  **Where is the middle?** — the typical value
2.  **How spread out is it?** — how much values vary

Answer both and you've described it. Everything else is detail.

### Mean vs median

Two ways to say "the middle":

-   The **mean** adds everything and divides — it feels every value,
    including extreme ones
-   The **median** is the middle value when sorted — it ignores *how*
    extreme the extremes are

::: callout-note
## Why the difference matters

Nine salespeople sell \$40,000 each and one sells \$2,000,000. The
**mean** is a figure nobody near actually sold. The **median** is
\$40,000.

Which better describes a typical salesperson?
:::

### The numbers for sales

Base R's `summary()` gives you the whole picture in one line, and it's
the quickest thing you can type:

```{r sales-summary}
summary(salespeople$sales)
```

Read the last column before anything else. **`NA's`** is a count of
missing values, and it's the reason for the `na.rm = TRUE` arguments
you're about to see. We'll come back to it properly at the end of the
chapter.

The tidyverse version is more typing but composes with everything else:

```{r sales-centre}
salespeople |>
  summarise(
    mean_sales   = mean(sales, na.rm = TRUE),
    median_sales = median(sales, na.rm = TRUE),
    sd_sales     = sd(sales, na.rm = TRUE)
  )
```

::: callout-tip
## What `na.rm = TRUE` is doing there

`sales` has a missing value in it, and R's summary functions **refuse
to guess**. Without `na.rm = TRUE` they hand back `NA` instead of a
number — or, in `quantile()`'s case, an outright error.

Treat `na.rm = TRUE` as a habit worth having by default whenever you
summarise real data. The last section of this chapter explains why
it's a habit rather than a fix.
:::

The **mean** is `r round(mean(salespeople$sales, na.rm = TRUE), 1)` and
the **median** is `r round(median(salespeople$sales, na.rm = TRUE), 1)`
(both in thousands of dollars).

### See it: the histogram

```{r sales-histogram}
ggplot(salespeople, aes(x = sales)) +
  geom_histogram(bins = 30, fill = "#122a52", colour = "white") +
  geom_vline(xintercept = mean(salespeople$sales, na.rm = TRUE),
             colour = "#3a4048", linewidth = 1) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = median(salespeople$sales, na.rm = TRUE),
             colour = "#3a4048", linewidth = 1, linetype = "dashed") +
  labs(
    title = "Distribution of sales",
    subtitle = "Solid line = mean, dashed line = median",
    x = "Sales (thousands of dollars)", y = "Number of salespeople"
  )
```

### What the shape tells you

Most salespeople cluster in the middle, with a **tail of high
performers** stretching to the right — that tail is exactly why the
mean sits *above* the median.

::: callout-important
When the mean and median disagree, the data is **skewed** — and you
should report both.
:::

### Spread: two measures

-   **Standard deviation (SD)** — roughly the typical distance from the
    mean. It pairs with the mean, and like the mean it feels every
    extreme value.
-   **Interquartile range (IQR)** — the range covering the middle 50%
    of the data, from the 25th to the 75th percentile. It pairs with
    the median, and like the median it shrugs off extremes.

```{r sales-spread}
salespeople |>
  summarise(
    sd  = sd(sales, na.rm = TRUE),
    q25 = quantile(sales, probs = 0.25, na.rm = TRUE),
    q75 = quantile(sales, probs = 0.75, na.rm = TRUE),
    iqr = IQR(sales, na.rm = TRUE)
  )
```

Note the pairing. If you report a **mean**, report an **SD** next to
it; if you report a **median**, report the **IQR**. Mixing them —
a median with a standard deviation — is a small tell that someone
reached for whichever number came to hand.

### The boxplot

```{r sales-boxplot}
ggplot(salespeople, aes(y = sales)) +
  geom_boxplot(fill = "#122a52", alpha = 0.8) +
  labs(title = "Sales — boxplot", y = "Sales (thousands of dollars)", x = NULL) +
  theme(axis.text.x = element_blank())
```

